21:17:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why it seems that way! Today, transplants are so common and successful that it really does look like the body just naturally accepts the new part once the surgery is done. But the reason doctors know the body doesn't naturally accept foreign organs is because of what used to happen in the past, and what happens today if a patient stops taking their medicine. **1. The Historical Proof** In the early days of transplant surgery in the 1950s and 60s, surgeons were perfectly capable of sewing a new kidney or heart into a patient. The surgeries were technically successful, and blood flowed into the new organs perfectly. But within days, the organs would swell, turn black, and die. The recipient's immune system destroyed them every single time. It wasn't until the 1980s, when a powerful anti-rejection drug called *cyclosporine* was introduced, that transplants actually started working long-term. **2. The Medication Proof** Today, if a person with a successful heart, liver, or kidney transplant decides to stop taking their daily anti-rejection pills, their body will almost immediately wake up, recognize the organ's different DNA, and start destroying it. This is a medical emergency called "acute rejection." So, you are absolutely right that organs *do* get accepted and function beautifully in a new body! It’s just that this acceptance isn't a natural biological process—it is an ongoing medical miracle. The body is being constantly "tricked" into accepting the organ by medications working quietly behind the scenes every single day.
